---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import HeroText from '~/components/widgets/HeroText.astro';
import SocialProof from '~/components/widgets/SocialProof.astro';
import { CORE, SURFACES, type Product } from '~/data/products';
import { WAITLIST_HREF } from '~/navigation';

const metadata = {
  title: 'About',
  description:
    'DNA Codes builds a living operating model for teams of humans and agents, and DNA, the open description language it runs on.',
};

const detail = (s: Product) => `: ${s.description.toLowerCase()} (${s.status.toLowerCase()})`;
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <HeroText tagline="About" title="We are building the operating model your software is missing." />

  <section class="px-4 sm:px-6 mx-auto max-w-3xl pb-8">
    <div class="prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert prose-headings:font-heading prose-headings:tracking-tight">
      <p>
        Every business already has an operating model. It lives in people's heads, in onboarding docs, in a wiki nobody
        trusts and in the permission checks scattered through its code. DNA Codes exists to write that model down once,
        in a form both people and software can read, and then run on it.
      </p>

      <h2>What we build</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>DNA</strong> is an open description language for business systems. It captures who your business deals with
        and in what capacity, the things it manages, the operations it performs and the rules that govern them. The language,
        its schemas and its adapters are published on npm under the <code>@dna-codes</code> scope and developed in the open
        on <a href="https://github.com/dna-codes">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Operations</strong> is the product built on it. At its core is <a href={CORE.href}>{CORE.name}</a>:&#32;
        {CORE.description.toLowerCase()}. Around that core sit surfaces, one for each place your software meets
        somebody:
      </p>
      <ul>
        {
          SURFACES.map((s) => (
            <li>
              {s.planned ? <strong>{s.name}</strong> : <a href={s.href}>{s.name}</a>}
              {detail(s)}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>

      <h2>Why it matters now</h2>
      <p>
        The future of work is humans and agents working side by side. An agent given a broad API key has no idea which
        operations it is allowed to perform or whose authority it is acting under. A model that names every role, every
        operation and every rule gives agents the same job description and the same limits a person would have, and
        keeps a record of what each one did.
      </p>
      <p>
        We write about the thinking behind this on <a href="/blog">the blog</a>, starting with the series on the
        abstraction layers between intent and execution that today's software skips.
      </p>

      <h2>Where we are</h2>
      <p>
        Operations is pre-release. We are working with design partners whose work has nothing to do with software, which
        is the point: an operating model has to hold up in any domain. If that sounds like your team, <a
          href={WAITLIST_HREF}>join the waitlist</a
        > or <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SocialProof />
</Layout>
